The smallest targeted resolver correction is to match the complete civil timestamp, including seconds and milliseconds, before classifying a candidate as an exact time or fold. Both the site's current resolver and the delivered SDK `0.1.1-rc.5` resolver currently compare only `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM`; they reproduce the same subminute-gap and false-fold flags.

1. Preserve the existing strict date/time/zone guards and the explicit `setUTCFullYear` construction of the typed Gregorian date. Keep the input contract `HH:MM`, meaning exactly zero seconds and milliseconds.
2. Add seconds and three fractional-second digits to the internal wall formatter, and compare each candidate with `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:00.000`, preserving the existing astronomical-year/era conversion. Normalize offset-derived candidate arithmetic to integer milliseconds with `Math.round(offsetMinutes * 60_000)` before constructing the candidate. Keep the returned offset as the actual Intl offset at that chosen instant.
3. Retain the current candidate-offset sampling and policies in this bounded fix: one exact candidate is ordinary; multiple distinct exact candidate instants form a fold and select the earlier instant; no exact candidates uses the existing forward-gap policy. Assert the postconditions in regression tests: ordinary/fold candidates reproduce the full typed civil timestamp; a reported gap has a positive actual wall shift; `lmt` reflects the actual chosen offset.

An alternative is to compare `candidateUtcMs + actualOffsetMs === typedWallMs` directly. That is mathematically equivalent for an integer-millisecond candidate and an actual offset returned by Intl. The reviewer-only arithmetic model was cross-checked against independently formatted Gregorian wall fields including seconds and milliseconds 834 times. The formatted comparator is recommended for the smallest structural change: it retains the current wall-format validation rather than removing it and relying solely on offset parsing. Do not round or truncate the final wall-clock comparison to a minute.

This fixes the discovered classification defects, subject to the existing sampled-offset coverage assumption. Exact comparison cannot discover an offset omitted by all three samples. The delivered experiment includes a clearly labeled synthetic two-transition model that misses an earlier fold even with exact comparison. No matching actual IANA close-transition failure was found here: the host TZif seed inventory contains 40,641 changed-offset transitions across 599 zone files, with its shortest adjacent interval 601,200 seconds (about 6.96 days). That host inventory is version 2026c, while executed Node Intl is tzdb 2026a, so it is not proof of all Intl history or all future runtimes. A general transition-discovery algorithm is separate architecture work; adding an arbitrary denser sample is not a completeness proof.

Meaningful verification for the correction:

- Add exact regression assertions to both site and SDK timezone suites for Manaus `1914-01-01 00:00`: `04:00:04Z`, offset -240, `dst-gap`; `00:01` and unknown-time `12:00` preserve their current instants and have no false `dst-fold`. Include a non-integer final offset, such as Caracas `1890-01-01 00:00`, and the 56-second Dawson Creek gap.
- Preserve a real subminute fold: Denver `1883-11-18 12:00` has two exact candidates four seconds apart and must still select `18:59:56Z`, with `dst-fold` and `lmt`. Test immediately neighboring input minutes to reject false folds outside that interval.
- Retain modern New York hour-gap/hour-fold, Lord Howe half-hour-gap/half-hour-fold, Apia whole-day gap, Mexico City 1907 LMT seconds, year 0000 leap day and cross-year fixed offset, years 0099/0100, invalid leap dates, invalid times, suffixed strings, and missing/invalid zones. Do not broaden the API date range as part of this fix.
- Run both actual resolver implementations on the retained finite transition/control table, then independent native-browser controls. Compare UTC instants, actual offsets and flags—not just successful return or self-roundtrip. Record Node/browser/ICU/tzdb and artifact/source identity. Current baseline evidence: site and installed SDK agree in all 278 controls; the experimental precision model changes flags in 140 controls, changes zero instants, and introduces no negative gap shifts. Eight existing invalid-input controls reject in both actual implementations. These are model findings, not a tested product patch.
- After a real patch, calculate bounded actual charts/receipts for the corrected gap and fold fixtures. Verify truthful context is accepted, replay instant/coordinates/requested houses are unchanged, and unknown time retains `local-noon` plus absent houses. Also run affected date-resolution consumers and required package/site checks. Keep the export admission fallback until its own release criteria explicitly permit narrowing it.

Exact matching is insufficient for unknown-time date endpoints. `localDateEndpointsUtc` currently reuses a forward-shifted midnight as if it were the first existing instant of a date. Actual supported-range counterexample: in `America/Toronto`, `1919-03-31` begins at `04:30Z` (local `00:30` after a gap from the previous date's `23:30`), but the helper starts `05:00Z` (local `01:00`). It omits the first 30 minutes. The prior date's current endpoint also includes those 30 minutes of the following date. The seconds-precision change preserves those incorrect endpoint instants, so this must be a separate civil-date-boundary correction, with a distinct start-of-date policy rather than changing birth-time gap resolution.

The endpoint follow-up should also define an explicit empty-date outcome for Apia `2011-12-30`, whose current result has `end = start - 1 ms`; dependent uncertainty/Registry claims must handle absence coherently. Its rollover helper uses `Date.UTC`, so `0000-01-01` currently ends in year 1900. That is a library-contract concern outside the chart UI's 1800–2199 supported range, not a claim the UI accepts year 0000. Preserve local-noon reference semantics independently of these endpoint corrections.

